  Recommendation: **Push to the existing otk-assistant repo** ✅

  This is the standard industry practice and the better choice for several reasons:

  ✅ Why Keep Docker Configs in the Same Repo:

  1. Single Source of Truth
    • Code and its deployment configuration stay together
    • Anyone cloning the repo can immediately see how to run it
  2. Easier Maintenance
    • When you update application code, you can update Docker configs in the same commit
    • No version mismatch issues between app and Docker configs
    • Clear history of what changed together
  3. Better Developer Experience
    • New contributors can clone and run immediately
    • Documentation is co-located with the code
    • README can reference Docker setup directly
  4. Simpler CI/CD
    • Build pipelines have everything they need in one place
    • Easier to set up GitHub Actions or other CI
    • Testing and deployment are straightforward
  5. Industry Standard
    • This is how 99% of modern applications are structured
    • Examples: Any major project on GitHub (Spring Boot samples, React apps, Node.js projects)


  ❌ When You'd Use a Separate Repo:

  Only in these specific scenarios:
  • Infrastructure-as-Code repo managing dozens of microservices
  • Kubernetes/Helm charts deployed separately from app code
  • Multi-repo architecture where one deployment config manages many apps
  • Corporate infrastructure team maintaining deployment separately


  💡 Recommendation:

  Just push to your existing `otk-assistant` repo on the `dbloader` branch (or a new docker-setup branch if you prefer). The Docker files are:
  • Small in size (configs, scripts, docs)
  • Directly related to running OTK Assistant
  • Useful for anyone using your application

  This keeps everything simple, maintainable, and follows best practices. Your repo structure would be exactly like any professional modern application.

